From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Mon, 28 Nov 94 16:52:45 EST
Subject: [#94-11-467] COMPUTERS: the persistence of C 

Trying to predict computer language popularity in fifteen years is difficult
in my opinion. First, you need to ask how you are measuring popularity.  You
can use `lines written' or `lines executed' or some other measure.

Nick Szabo's prediction about C does not seem to rely much on the concept of
`information distance'. He appears to be claiming that C fits a niche which
will remain important in fifteen years and that inertia will prevent it from
being replaced by a comparable language. This is plausible to me but the
niche ``efficient and also high-level'' may be changing. In C you can
declare a variable as a register. Does this make sense with future
sophisticated compilers for chips that may be VLIW, heavily pipelined,
super-scalar. In fifteen years there will probably be many functional units
on a chip. A future efficient language may be one which exposes parallelism
effectively so that the many units on the chip can be kept busy.  C has a
conventional thread of control.

Looking back in time `Why did C become popular?' UNIX was given free/cheaply
to universities by ATT.  OS courses could be taught using UNIX because
source code in C was available.  UNIX multiplied.  Many C hackers were
created. There is more serendipity to this story (e.g. the actions of ATT)
then logical trend in my opinion.  Could the rise of C be predicted over a
15 year timeframe?

What are the popular languages now?  There are candidates that are not
usually mentioned.  In terms of lines written or executed maybe a
spreadsheet language (Excel macros, etc) is most popular?  What will be
popular in the future? Below are some candidate classes of languages:

Conventional (C, etc.)
Legacy (Fortran, Cobol)
Military (Ada)
Spreadsheet language (Excel macros, etc) is most popular?
High level database/development language (PowerBuilder, etc)
Bill Gates obsession (Basic, Visual Basic)
Functional language (Haskel, etc.)
Mathematically oriented language (Mathematica, Maple)
Object oriented (C++, Objective C, Smalltalk)
and other classes I am forgetfully omitting.

Some emerging classes:

Network oriented agent language (TeleScript)
Multimedia scripting (Hyperscript, ScriptX)

Embryonic classes:

Virtual reality oriented language manipulating objects/behaviors/layout
Developer language for interactive set top boxes
                     gfs

